Title:
BOF Collection (beune/bof_collection)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository is a collection of Cobalt Strike Beacon Object Files (BOFs) focused on remote Windows enumeration and a kernel privilege escalation PoC. Several BOFs leverage the Remote Registry Protocol (RRP) to infer HKLM registry key existence, check remote disk paths, and read exact Windows patch level, relying on subtle error-code/info-leak behaviors. It also includes an SMB2-based named pipe enumerator that authenticates via SSPI and lists pipes from the IPC$ share without .NET dependencies. Utility BOFs include a memory range helper for Beacon debugging and a safe-ish file “cat” supporting UTF-8/UTF-16 and UNC paths. The repo additionally contains a CVE-2019-16098 exploit chain abusing RTCore64.sys primitives plus a kASLR leak to steal a SYSTEM token. It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters building low-footprint post-exploitation capabilities and for researchers studying RRP/SMB enumeration and driver exploitation tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- Cobalt Strike BOF development (C, Beacon API)
- Remote Registry Protocol (RRP) abuse and information leaks
- SMB2 client implementation and named pipe enumeration over IPC$
- SSPI/Windows integrated authentication
- Windows patch level discovery via HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion
- Kernel exploitation: kASLR leak + RTCore64.sys R/W primitive (CVE-2019-16098)

Use Cases:
- Remote host recon: enumerate HKLM key presence without full registry access
- Identify remote file/folder existence via RRP side effects (with artifact risk)
- Determine exact Windows build/patch level for exploitability assessment
- Enumerate remote named pipes over SMB2 for lateral movement planning
- Debug/inspect Beacon memory layout during BOF development
- Local privilege escalation research/testing using RTCore64.sys-based primitives

Keywords:
Beacon Object File, BOF, Cobalt Strike, beacon.h, MinGW, boflink, Remote Registry Protocol, RRP, winreg named pipe, HKLM, Reg* APIs, information leak, SMB2, IPC$, named pipes, SSPI, TCP/445, patch level enumeration, CVE-2019-16098, RTCore64.sys, kASLR leak, kernel exploit, token stealing, SYSTEM token